      <div class="kicker">Our story</div>
      <h2>She sold bread to make rent. She built an institution.</h2>
      <p>Sybil Bernard-Kerrutt immigrated from Guyana and worked as a hairdresser in New York. To make rent and support her family, she started selling bread to friends. The bread became a business. The business became Sybil&rsquo;s.</p>
      <p>In 1976 she opened her first bakery in Jamaica, Queens. The flagship followed on Liberty Ave &mdash; that striking yellow-and-turquoise triangular building at 133rd St that you can spot from blocks away, in the heart of Little Guyana. Sybil passed in 2000; her family runs it still, on the original recipes.</p>
      <div class="timeline">
        <div class="tl"><b>Start</b><p><strong>Bread from home.</strong> Sybil sells loaves to friends &mdash; word travels the way it only does when the bread is that good.</p></div>
        <div class="tl"><b>1976</b><p><strong>First bakery opens</strong> in Jamaica, Queens &mdash; the original location, still serving on Hillside Ave.</p></div>
        <div class="tl"><b>Flagship</b><p><strong>Liberty Ave, South Richmond Hill.</strong> The triangular yellow-and-turquoise landmark, near JFK &mdash; for many Guyanese visitors, the first stop in New York and the last stop out.</p></div>
        <div class="tl"><b>Today</b><p><strong>Family-owned, line out the door</strong> &mdash; even on a weekday afternoon. Same pepperpot, same pine tarts, same plait bread baked in-house.</p></div>
